course: english
lesson: 44
title: Writing to inform, instruct and persuade
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every piece of writing has a job, and the job decides its shape: instructions tell you how, reports and explanations tell you what and why, and persuasive writing tries to change your mind.
explain: Instructions are like a recipe card on the fridge: a title saying what you will make, a list of what you need, then numbered steps that start with bossy verbs ("Wet the brush. Squeeze a pea-sized blob of toothpaste."), so a story about your morning will not get anyone's teeth clean.
explain: A report on penguins is sorted by topic, not by time, under headings like "Habitat" and "Diet", in the present tense; a newspaper report tells who, what, where, when and why, in the past tense, with a headline and a quote.
explain: Persuasion uses reasons, evidence and feelings, with a polite voice: "Dear Council, the park is boss, don't close it or else!" sounds like a threat from a Liverpool lad ("boss" means great), while "Over 200 children play here every week" gives the council something to think about.
explain: A balanced argument is the fair cousin of persuasion: it gives the reasons on both sides, for example of banning school uniforms, before the writer says what they think at the end.
fact: Instructions have a goal, a list of things needed, and numbered steps in order.
fact: Instructions use imperative (bossy) verbs at the start of steps: mix, cut, press, brush.
fact: A non-chronological report organises facts by topic, not by time, usually in the present tense with headings.
fact: A recount retells events in time order, usually in the past tense, using time words like first, then, after that and finally.
fact: A newspaper report has a headline, answers the 5 Ws (who, what, where, when, why), is in the past tense and often includes quotes.
fact: A persuasive letter states what the writer wants, gives reasons and evidence, and stays polite.
fact: Persuasive devices include rhetorical questions, facts and numbers, strong adjectives and speaking directly to the reader as "you".
fact: An explanation shows how or why something happens, using cause-and-effect words such as because, so and as a result.
fact: A balanced argument presents points for and against, then gives a conclusion.
fact: A diary entry is written in the first person (I, we), usually in the past tense, and shares feelings as well as events.
fact: Adverts use persuasive language and slogans, sometimes slang to sound friendly to young people; "bussin'" in Gen Z slang means really good.
fact: In Victorian times, some children worked as chimney sweeps' climbing boys, a dangerous job; a law of 1875 finally stopped it.
exercise: Year 4 are asked for instructions for brushing teeth. Sam writes a story about his morning. What is missing? | Numbered steps that tell the reader what to do | A happy ending where his teeth sparkle like stars | A list of the characters and where they live | A rhyme at the end of every single line
exercise: Which is the best first step of tooth-brushing instructions? | Wet your toothbrush under the tap. | My toothbrush is blue and I like it a lot. | Once upon a time there was a toothbrush. | Teeth are hard white things in your mouth.
exercise: What kind of verb starts most instruction steps? | Imperative (bossy) verbs | Verbs in the past tense, like "brushed" | Modal verbs, like "might" or "could" | Verbs ending in -ing, like "brushing"
exercise: A teacher asks for a non-chronological report on penguins. How should it be organised? | By topic, with headings | By the time of day, from morning to night | As a story told by one penguin | As a list of rhymes about ice
exercise: Year 5 write a persuasive letter to save the park. What makes it work? | Reasons, evidence and a polite tone | Shouting in capitals so the council notices | As many threats as possible to scare them | A long story about a dragon in the park
exercise: Liam from Liverpool drafts "Dear Council, the park is boss, don't close it or else!" What should he change? | Use standard words and drop the threat | Add more slang so they know he's real | Put the whole letter in capital letters | Make "or else" bigger and underline it
exercise: Year 6 write a newspaper report on the school fire drill. Which belongs in it? | A headline and the 5 Ws | A rhyming chorus | A list of steps with bossy verbs | A "once upon a time" opening
exercise: What are the 5 Ws? | Who, what, where, when, why | Write, wait, wonder, walk, win | Words, weeks, weather, wind, water | Why, why, why, why, why
exercise: Kai writes "I went to the zoo and saw stuff and it was good." How could he improve his recount? | Use time order and precise details | Change it to the future tense | Remove all the words about the zoo | Turn it into instructions with numbers
exercise: The teacher asks for a diary entry as a Victorian chimney sweep. Which line fits best? | Today I climbed three chimneys and my knees are raw. | Step 1: climb the chimney. Step 2: sweep it out. | Chimney sweeps are workers who clean chimneys. | Dear Council, please build a new chimney.
exercise: Grace, 9, says "This advert is so bussin', you gotta buy it." What does "bussin'" mean? | Really good | Very noisy | Too pricey | Bus-shaped
exercise: Why might an advert for children use slang like Grace's? | To sound friendly to young people | Because adverts are not allowed to use Standard English | Because slang is the only language that persuades | Because the company forgot how to spell properly
exercise: Year 5 write an explanation of how rain forms. Which linking words fit best? | because, so, as a result | once upon a time, suddenly | firstly, dear sir, yours | lol, tbh, ngl
exercise: What does a balanced argument on banning uniforms do? | Gives both sides, then a conclusion | Gives only the reasons for banning them | Gives only the reasons against banning them | Tells a story about a uniform that was lost
exercise: Which tense is usual for a report on penguins? | Present | Future | Past progressive | Present perfect, always
exercise: Which sentence is a rhetorical question used to persuade? | Would you like your park to become a car park? | What time does the park close on Sundays? | Where is the nearest park to my house? | How many swings are there in the park?
test: Your mate goes "all writing's the same, bruv, just write loads." What is the truth? | Each kind of writing has its own job and shape | Writing more words always gets more marks | Only stories count as real writing | Instructions and reports are the same as poems
test: ★★★ Which set of features belongs to a newspaper report and NOT to instructions? | Headline, past tense, quotes from witnesses | Numbered steps, bossy verbs, list of equipment | Present tense, "you", a goal at the top | Time order, short sentences, a title
test: In a cartoon, a recipe says "Then the brave egg cried and ran away from the pan." What has gone wrong? | It turned instructions into a story | It has too many bossy verbs | It is a perfect recipe for eggs | It is written in the present tense
test: What would happen if instructions for building a model were in the wrong order? | The reader could get stuck or build it wrong | The model would build itself correctly anyway | The reader would enjoy it more as a mystery | Nothing, because order never matters in instructions
test: A grandad in Glasgow, dry: "Yer letter to the council says 'ye numpties'. That'll work a treat." ("Numpties" means silly people.) What is he really saying? | Insults will not persuade the council | The council loves being called numpties | The letter is perfect and very polite | He wants more Scottish words in it
test: ★★★ Which sentence uses evidence, not just opinion, to persuade? | Last year 300 people signed a petition to keep the park. | The park is the best place in the whole world, honestly. | Everybody knows that parks are better than car parks. | Closing the park would be a really, really bad idea.
test: A Gen Alpha kid writes a report: "Penguins are lowkey goated." ("Goated" means the greatest.) In a report, which sentence works better? | Emperor penguins can dive deeper than 500 metres. | Penguins are goated, no cap, they slay. | Penguins are the best, I think so anyway. | Penguins are lowkey goated in a big way.
test: ★★★ "First, we lined up. Then the alarm rang. Finally, Mr Ali counted us." What kind of writing is this most likely? | A recount | Instructions | A persuasive letter | A balanced argument
test: A cousin in Texas says "Y'all should write the rules for the game real clear." What kind of text are rules for a game? | Instructions | A diary | A poem | A recount
test: Your sister, sarky: "Your 'balanced' argument has six reasons for and zero against. Very fair." What is wrong? | It gives only one side | It has too many paragraphs | It has a headline | It uses the past tense
test: Which is the best headline for the fire drill report? | Pupils Out in Two Minutes as Alarm Sounds | It Was a Day at School and Things Happened | My Diary: Tuesday | How to Make a Fire Alarm
exam: Identify the principal feature of instructional writing. | A sequence of numbered steps employing imperative verbs | A narrative recounted from a single viewpoint | A collection of rhyming couplets | A balanced discussion of opposing views
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a non-chronological report? | Information organised by topic, customarily in the present tense | Events recounted strictly in order of time, in the past tense | A narrative featuring a protagonist and an antagonist | A letter addressed to a person in authority
exam: Select the response consistent with established conventions of persuasive correspondence. | Reasons and evidence presented in a courteous register | Threats presented in an emphatic register | Slang presented in an informal register | Rhymes presented in a lyrical register
exam: Ascertain the five interrogatives customarily addressed in a newspaper report. | Who, what, where, when and why | How, how much, how many, how far and how long | Which, whose, whom, whither and whence | If, but, and, or and so
